theory.intro:
To write a curve in **parametric form**, introduce a parameter $t$ (or $\theta$) that gives $x$ and $y$ separately. For lines and parabolas, substitute the given $x$ into the Cartesian equation. Circles use $\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta = 1$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes, calc}
\coordinate (C) at (1.3,0.9);
\coordinate (P) at (3.138,2.443);
\coordinate (F) at (3.138,0.9);
\draw[<->] (-2.0,0) -- (4.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-2.4) -- (0,3.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (C) circle (2.4);
\draw[dashed] (C) -- (F);
\draw[dashed] (F) -- (P);
\draw (C) -- (P) node[midway, above left] {$r$};
\coordinate (f1) at ($(F)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\coordinate (f2) at ($(F)!0.3cm!(P)$);
\draw (f1) -- ($(f1)+(f2)-(F)$) -- (f2);
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.75cm, angle eccentricity=1.9] {angle = F--C--P};
\fill (C) circle (2pt) node[left] {$(a,b)$};
\fill (P) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$P(x,y)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
